  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe core idea is to combine constrained optimization with dimensionally-reduced coordinate descent to break local optima traps. Unlike previous methods using generic continuous optimization, we first generate a coarse-step Fourier basis to capture global structure, then refine via adaptive projection across multiple resolutions. This leverages the observation that optimal solutions exhibit self-similarity patterns akin to quasi-periodic functions with specific symmetry. The major limitation is potential sensitivity to initial conditions, but multi-scale refinement at several resolutions (600, 500, 700) with \u03b4-adaptive projection ensures robustness against trapped solutions. The method avoids direct global search by embedding it into local optimization heuristics, focusing on constraint preservation while respecting the maximum overlap minimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Objective: C5 = max_{k \u2208 Z} \u222b [0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Constraints: \n  1. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n  2. \u222b [0,2] h(x) dx = 1\n- Optimization: Variable h \u2208 \u211d^{n}, with:\n  \u2225h\u2225_\u221e \u2264 1\n  \u2211h_i = n/2\n- Discretization: h \u2208 [0,1]^{n} for n \u2208 {600,500,700}; dx = 2/n\n- Implementation uses coordinate-based descent with Gershgorin projection: Proj(h) = clip(h,0,1) + \u03b1(\u2211_i h_i - n/2) * h for 0<\u03b1<1\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a. Initialize h(600) via Fourier_wake(n=600, seed), h(500)=interpolate to target length while preserving integrals\n   b. Project Feasibility(h):\n        While \u2225h_clip - h_old\u2225 > tolerance:\n            h_clip = clip(h,0,1)\n            \u03bb = sign(\u2211h_clip - n/2)\n            h = h_old + \u03bb * (clip_range) * (clip_sum_diff - d_clip_diff) + \u03b4_adaptive for multidimensional weighting\n   c. Core Loop:\n        For resolution n in {700,500,600} sorted descending by n:\n            Solve h = coordinate_descent(h_init, n, budget_breakdown=...)\n            Use relative improvement to select winner resolution for final copies\n        Stage1: Coarse Coordinate Descent (4 phases):\n            1.1. Relax h to Laplacian-smoothed function with periodic boundary consideration\n            1.2. Minimize max_overlap using damped Krylov iteration with \u03b4-insensitive ob optimization\n            1.3. Project cost-sensitive components to maintain stability\n            1.4. Post-process to enforce positivity and unit integral via iterative projection\n        Stage2: Coarse-Guided Local Search:\n            h = optimize_gradient(h, n, step_decay=transform_problem())\n        Stage3: Final polishing with adaptive sampling from continuous high-loss regions\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Default resolution weights: weights = {600:1,500:0.8,700:0.9}\n   - Projection adapton:a\n        p_proj_window = 25\n        p_proj_steps = {small_problem:200, medium:300, large_res:400}\n   - Coordinate Descent uses:\n        coordinate_restriction = {'small_problem':20, 'medium':30, 'large_res':50}\n        variation_type = switch_neighborhood(start_iter)\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness:\n   Time budget: Up to 1100 seconds\n   Schedule:\n        Stage1:\u05d9\u05d1\u05d5\u05e8\u05da_RT_seconds = min(300+700*n/len,n) parallel processors\n        Schedule cooling factor: 0.995 at higher iterations\n        Projection cooling: 0.8 at resolution 500 and above\n        Hardware: Single CPU assumed\n   Fallback on failure: Mixture-of-experts interpolation method on [0.2,0.8] spaced query points near the period- twinx boundary\n   Budget: 3 moderator samples at $\\alpha=0.5,0.7,1.0$ with exponential decay per iteration\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   Pre-check candidate solution:\n        assert len(h) in {500,600,700}\n        assert all(0<=x<=1 for x in h)\n        assert abs(sum(h)*dx - 1) < 1e-6\n        Compute actual C5 from np.correlate as in the evaluator\n   Return format:\n        h_values clipped to float32\n        c5_bound = minimized max_overlap from the correlation\n        n_points = h_values.length\n\n   Developer must rigorously ensure:\n        Range errors in correlation calculation are clipped to 0-1\n        Division and correlation out-of-bounds are handled with signal padding\n        NaN outputs are replaced by budget-floor value",
